// The ownership marks: a value the program allocates and must give back, and
// what a native function does with one it is handed.
import ir "sngl:internal/ir"

// Declares that a value of the marked struct is something the program owns and
// must give back, and names the function that does: `release` takes one value
// of the type and returns nothing. The compiler releases each value it can
// prove is no longer reachable, at the narrowest of four lifetimes -- the
// statement, the node, the application, or never, once it is moved.
//
// Nothing is owned by its name. A C string, a pixbuf and a program's own
// handle are owned because their declarations say so.
func owned[T any](release func(T)) ir.Macro

// Marks a parameter of a native function that keeps a pointer to its argument
// after it returns, while the caller still owns it: the value lives as long as
// the node that made the call, rather than the statement.
func retains() ir.Macro

// Marks a parameter of a native function that takes its argument: the callee
// releases it, so the caller never does.
func moves() ir.Macro
